# Turning a circle inside out {
  :label: inversion
  :eyebrow: Note
}

:config: {
  :numbering: none
  :citation-style: author-parens-year
  :override-date: 8 November 2026 · 4 minute read
}
::

Fix a circle of radius 1 with center $O$. Inversion in this circle sends a point $p$, other than $O$, to the point $q$ on the ray from $O$ through $p$ at distance $1/|p|$ from $O$. Points near the circle move a little. Points near $O$ are sent far away, and far points come in close to $O$. Points on the circle stay where they are. Inverting twice gives $p$ back, so the points come in pairs.

:html: {
  :path: figs/inversion.html
  :nonum:
}
:caption: Inversion in the circle of radius 1 with center $O$. The point $q$ lies on the ray from
$O$ through $p$, at distance $1/|p|$, so $|p|\,|q| = 1$. Each circle shown appears with its image in
the same color, the image dashed.
::

Drag $p$, and $q$ follows it. Drag $q$, and $p$ follows. Bring either one to the circle and the two meet.

The readout under the figure says $|p|\,|q| = 1$ wherever $p$ goes. That is only the definition, but watching $q$ run off as $p$ nears the center shows what it costs: $O$ has no partner. The usual fix is to add one point at infinity and make it the partner of $O$. Inversion is then a one-to-one map of the plane with that point added, which is :aside:sphere,the Riemann sphere::.

:aside: {:label: sphere} In complex numbers, with $O$ at $0$, inversion is $z \mapsto 1/\bar z$. Followed by the reflection $z \mapsto \bar z$ it becomes $z \mapsto 1/z$. ::

Now turn on the two circles, and drag either one to move it. The teal circle always passes through $O$, and its image is the dashed teal line. Points of that circle near $O$ are sent far away, so its image must run off to infinity, and the circles that do that are lines. The plum circle misses $O$, and its image is again a circle. So inversion sends circles and lines to circles and lines. It does not send centers to centers: the inverse of the small circle's center is not the center of its image.

The image of the teal circle is a line, and not just a curve that runs off to infinity. A proof follows, with a figure of its own.

:theorem: {
  :label: thm-line
  :title: Circles through the center
}

  Inversion sends a circle through $O$, with $O$ left out, onto a line that misses $O$.

::

:html: {
  :label: fig-line
  :path: figs/through-line.html
  :nonum:
}
:caption: The circle through $O$, with $A$ opposite $O$ and $A'$ the inverse of $A$. As $P$ goes
around the circle, its inverse $P'$ stays on the dashed line through $A'$. The triangles $OPA$ and
$OA'P'$ are similar, and each has a right angle, at $P$ and at $A'$.
::

:proof:

  :step: {:label: stp-line-setup}

    :let: $C$ be a circle through $O$, $A$ the point of $C$ opposite $O$, and $A'$ the inverse
    of $A$. Let $L$ be the line through $A'$ at right angles to $OA$ ::.

  ::

  :step: {:label: stp-line-on}

    :claim: For every point $P$ of $C$ other than $O$, its inverse $P'$ lies on $L$. ::

    :p:

      :step: {:label: stp-line-a} If $P = A$, then $P' = A'$, which lies on $L$. From now on
      $P$ is neither $O$ nor $A$. ::

      :step: {:label: stp-line-thales} The angle $OPA$ is a right angle, since $OA$ is a
      diameter of $C$. ::

      :step: {:label: stp-line-similar} The triangles $OPA$ and $OA'P'$ are similar. They share
      the angle at $O$, and $|OP|\,|OP'| = 1 = |OA|\,|OA'|$ gives $|OP| / |OA'| = |OA| / |OP'|$. ::

      :step: By :ref:stp-line-similar::, the angle $OA'P'$ equals the angle $OPA$, a right angle
      by :ref:stp-line-thales::. So $P'$ lies on the line through $A'$ at right angles to $OA$,
      which is $L$. ::

    ::

  ::

  :step: {:label: stp-line-onto}

    :claim: Every point $X$ of $L$ is the inverse of a point of $C$. ::

    :p: $X$ lies on the same side as $A$ of the line through $O$ at right angles to $OA$, so the
    ray from $O$ through $X$ meets $C$ at a point $P$ other than $O$. By :ref:stp-line-on::, $P'$
    lies on $L$, and it lies on the same ray. The ray meets $L$ only at $X$, so $P' = X$. ::

  ::

  :step: $L$ misses $O$, since $|OA'| = 1 / |OA| > 0$. ::

::

This is what makes inversion useful. Two circles that touch at a point become two parallel lines after an inversion centered there, and a question about circles squeezed between them becomes a question about circles between parallel lines, which is often easy. Many classical proofs about chains of touching circles go this way; the books below have several.

Inversion also :aside:liouville,keeps angles::. Two curves that cross at some angle have images that cross at the same angle, with the sense of turning reversed. The figure does not show this. It moves one point at a time, and an angle needs two curves through a point. No picture is known that makes this as plain as dragging $p$ makes $|p|\,|q| = 1$. Suggestions are welcome.

:aside: {:label: liouville} In three or more dimensions this has a strong converse, due to Liouville: every map that keeps angles is built from inversions in spheres, reflections and similarities. In the plane there are many more, for example every complex function with a nonzero derivative. ::

## Sources {
  :nonum:
  :label: sources
}

Needham, chapter 3, draws everything above and much more, with complex numbers. Coxeter, chapter 6, and Coxeter and Greitzer, chapter 5, do it with classical geometry and many exercises: :cite:needham, coxeter, coxetergreitzer::.

